(a) Purpose. The purpose of this article is to provide for the fair and equitable treatment of all persons involved in public purchasing by the City of Kingsland, to maximize the purchasing value of public funds in procurement, and to provide safeguards for maintaining a procurement system of quality and integrity.
(b) Application.
(1) This article applies to the procurement, including contracts, of supplies, services, and construction, entered into by the City of Kingsland after the effective date of this article, unless the parties agree to its application to contracts entered into prior to the effective date. This article shall apply to all expenditures of public funds by a public agency for public purchasing irrespective of the source of the funds. All purchases using public funds, including state and federal funds, must be necessary and a justifiable purchase to carry out the basic needs of the government. The difference in any voluntary upgrade from the basic needs shall be reimbursed by the purchaser. Upgrades occurring in, but not limited to, all travel related items, office and general supplies, capital outlays, repairs and maintenance, and services, etc., shall be reimbursed by the purchaser if not approved as necessary to meet the basic needs of the city.
(2) When the procurement involves the expenditure of federal assistance or contract funds, the procurement shall be conducted in accordance with any mandatory applicable federal law and regulations. Nothing in this article shall prevent any public agency from complying with the terms and conditions of any grant, gift, or bequest that is otherwise consistent with law.
(c) Duties of purchasing agent. The purchasing agent is responsible for ensuring that city departments comply with federal, state, and local statutes regulating competitive sealed bids, sealed proposals, professional services, cooperative purchases, emergency and sole-source purchases in a manner to yield the maximum value for each dollar spent. Other duties include, but are not limited to:
(1) Observe and enforce the policy and procedures as outlined in the City of Kingsland Purchasing Ordinance or as directed by the city manager and finance director or their designee;
(2) Advise and assist in the formulation of policies and procedures connected with the purchasing activities of the city and assist with conducting regular training sessions for employees involved in the purchasing process;
(3) Investigate and analyze research done in the field of purchasing by other governmental agencies and by private industry, in an effort to stay current on developments in the field of purchasing, price, market conditions and new products;
(4) Coordinate, organize, and assist departments in the specification writing process to ensure that specifications are written concisely and are not written in an exclusive manner;
(5) Join with other governmental agencies in cooperative purchasing plans when it is in the best interest of the city;
(6) Receive, open, and evaluate competitive solicitations and verify successful vendor;
(7) Prepare and coordinate with user departments staff reports recommending award of competitive solicitations for city council approval;
(8) Act as contract administrator for the city which includes, but is not limited to: monitoring time lines for completion and rebidding contract at renewal dates, documenting compliance and complaint issues and steps taken to address these issues with the vendor;
(9) Oversee contractor or vendor or designate appropriate department representative to ensure agreed upon procedures for services and goods are being received prior to approval of payment;
(10) Combine purchases of similar items whenever possible and practical with city departments and surrounding governmental entities, to allow for better pricing and to establish a more competitive atmosphere;
(11) Assist department heads in the disposition of surplus city property;
(12) For each purchase, maintain documentation providing evidence of which procurement methodology acceptable under this article was used to render decision. The amount of documentation will vary based on the procurement methodology utilized; however, the documentation should be sufficient enough to allow for the understanding as to why the award decision was made.
(Ord. No. 2015-10, 6-22-2015; Ord. No. 2019-04, 4-8-2019)

