(a) A contract may be awarded without competition when the purchasing agent determines in writing, after the user and purchasing agent conducts a good faith review of available sources, that there is only one source for the required supply, service, or construction item. The purchasing agent shall conduct negotiations, as appropriate, as to price, delivery, and terms. A record of sole source procurements shall be maintained as a public record and shall list each vendor's name, the amount and type of each contract, a listing of the item(s) procured under each contract, and the identification number of each contract file.
(b) If the item is unique and only available from a single source, then the sole source procurement standards would apply. They would also apply in these instances: (1) during a public emergency, (2) if written request has been made and approved by the federal or state pass-through entity, or (3) if competition is deemed inadequate after solicitation from a number of sources was attempted.
(c) Additionally, the following expenditures are exempted from the competitive purchase requirements of this article; however, the city manager may develop procedures to oversee all transactions made by the entire city:
(1) Real estate acquisitions;
(2) Costs contained within intergovernmental agreements;
(3) Dues, memberships, and professional subscriptions;
(4) Utility services including, but not limited to, water, sewer, electricity, and gas;
(5) Legal services;
(6) Legal and display advertisements (as required);
(7) Payments to professional services; such as, arbitrators, engineers, attorneys, etc.;
(8) Conferences, seminars, and classes;
(9) Software renewal/licensing/maintenance (currently in use);
(10) Reimbursements for valid, approved travel expenses as submitted on a travel expense report;
(11) Instructors or speakers for internal training events;
(12) Surplus property acquisitions from other governments or governmental agencies;
(13) Employee benefits payments;
(14) Fuel purchases at the pump; and,
(15) U.S. Postal Service, United Parcel Service, Federal Express, Airborne, or other delivery services.
(Ord. No. 2015-10, 6-22-2015; Ord. No. 2019-04, 4-8-2019)

